#!/usr/bin/env python3
# =============================================================================
# customer_material_forecast.py
# Standalone (no Streamlit / no UI) forecaster: one chosen model, every
# customer/material combination, wide date-column output for comparing
# against your own existing "locality forecast".
# =============================================================================
#
# WHAT THIS IS
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   The third of three related scripts:
#     - production_forecasting_mvp.py    = full interactive Streamlit app
#     - overall_forecast.py               = company-wide total only
#     - batch_forecast_comparison.py      = all 4 models + backtest accuracy,
#                                            per customer/material, in SQLite
#     - customer_material_forecast.py (this one) = ONE model you choose,
#       every customer/material combination, NO backtesting (by design --
#       you're comparing forecast numbers against your own predictions, not
#       scoring accuracy here), output as one wide Excel sheet: one row per
#       customer/material, one column per forecast date.
#
#   No backtesting means each segment only needs a single fit + forecast
#   instead of 8, which is what makes ~9,000+ segments practical to run in
#   one sitting. AutoARIMA/AutoETS/Seasonal Naive are additionally
#   vectorized across ALL segments in one call (statsforecast's native
#   panel forecast()) rather than looped one at a time -- the same trick
#   batch_forecast_comparison.py uses for its backtest.
#
# INSTALLATION
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   pip install pandas numpy openpyxl scikit-learn statsforecast scipy
#
# RUN
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   python customer_material_forecast.py --workbook "Sales Order 2024-26.xlsx" --model AutoARIMA
#
#   Flags (see --help for the full list):
#     --model {Seasonal Naive,AutoARIMA,AutoETS,HistGradientBoostingRegressor,Average}
#                                     which forecast to put in the output (default: Average,
#                                     which needs all 4 models computed -- slower than picking one)
#     --excel-out customer_material_forecast.xlsx
#     --horizon 21                   forecast horizon in days
#     --align-to-sunday              start the forecast on the next Sunday instead of tomorrow
#                                     (see overall_forecast.py's flag of the same name)
#     --thorough-arima / --fast-arima   AutoARIMA search depth (default: thorough here, since
#                                     skipping the backtest already saves ~8x -- see below)
#     --n-jobs -1                    CPU cores for the vectorized statsforecast call
#     --limit 50                     only process the first N segments (quick timing test)
#     --as-of YYYY-MM-DD             historical cutoff (default: today)
#
# ON ACCURACY VS. SPEED
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Because there's no backtest, AutoARIMA's default here is the THOROUGH
#   search (the other two scripts default to a faster reduced search,
#   because they pay that cost 8 times per segment for the backtest --
#   this script pays it once). If --model AutoARIMA or --model Average is
#   still too slow across your full segment count, --fast-arima trades some
#   accuracy for a ~5-6x speedup (measured in batch_forecast_comparison.py's
#   benchmarking). Seasonal Naive, AutoETS, and HistGradientBoostingRegressor
#   are all much cheaper per segment than AutoARIMA regardless of this flag
#   -- if runtime is tight, picking one of those instead of AutoARIMA/Average
#   is the bigger lever. Run with --limit 50 first to get a real per-segment
#   timing on your own hardware before committing to the full run.
#
# =============================================================================

